{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794567573321,"sessionID":"ses_e2b7162e1ffepACOlFctQOU9iv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call-5de7b678-ee51-4633-8baf-be1ddc5a8fb1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/record && python3 -c \"\nimport tempfile, os, json, hashlib, shutil\nimport record as R\n\n# Test that the new check works by mocking the sigstore verification\n# Let's trace through the code to ensure the logic is correct\n\nd = tempfile.mkdtemp()\ntry:\n    os.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'entries'))\n    os.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'anchors'))\n    \n    # Create 2 entries\n    e1 = {'seq': 1, 'prev': None, 'kind': 'plan', 'written_at': '2024-01-01T00:00:00Z', 'body': {'by': 'a', 'claim': 'c', 'test': 't', 'decision_rule': 'd'}}\n    raw1 = R.canonical(e1)\n    with open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000001.json'), 'wb') as f:\n        f.write(raw1)\n    \n    e2 = {'seq': 2, 'prev': hashlib.sha256(raw1).hexdigest(), 'kind': 'plan', 'written_at': '2024-01-01T00:00:00Z', 'body': {'by': 'a', 'claim': 'c2', 'test': 't', 'decision_rule': 'd'}}\n    raw2 = R.canonical(e2)\n    with open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000002.json'), 'wb') as f:\n        f.write(raw2)\n    \n    # Create a sigstore.json file (it will fail verification but we can see the flow)\n    # Actually, let's just verify the logic by reading the code\n    \n    # The key insight: after processing all anchors, ranges contains (from_seq, to_seq, commit) \n    # for each valid sigstore anchor. My fix checks that for each to_seq, the .ots file exists.\n    \n    print('The fix checks: for each range head (to_seq), anchors/{to_seq:06d}.ots must exist')\n    print('This is exactly what the bug report requested')\nfinally:\n    shutil.rmtree(d)\n\""},"output":"The fix checks: for each range head (to_seq), anchors/{to_seq:06d}.ots must exist\nThis is exactly what the bug report requested\n","metadata":{"output":"The fix checks: for each range head (to_seq), anchors/{to_seq:06d}.ots must exist\nThis is exactly what the bug report requested\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace/record && python3 -c \"\nimport tempfile, os, json, hashlib, shutil\nimport record as R\n\n# Test that the new check works by mocking the sigstore verification\n# Let's trace through the code to ensure the logic is correct\n\nd = tempfile.mkdtemp()\ntry:\n    os.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'entries'))\n    os.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'anchors'))\n    \n    # Create 2 entries\n    e1 = {'seq': 1, 'prev': None, 'kind': 'plan', 'written_at': '2024-01-01T00:00:00Z', 'body': {'by': 'a', 'claim': 'c', 'test': 't', 'decision_rule': 'd'}}\n    raw1 = R.canonical(e1)\n    with open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000001.json'), 'wb') as f:\n        f.write(raw1)\n    \n    e2 = {'seq': 2, 'prev': hashlib.sha256(raw1).hexdigest(), 'kind': 'plan', 'written_at': '2024-01-01T00:00:00Z', 'body': {'by': 'a', 'claim': 'c2', 'test': 't', 'decision_rule': 'd'}}\n    raw2 = R.canonical(e2)\n    with open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000002.json'), 'wb') as f:\n        f.write(raw2)\n    \n    # Create a sigstore.json file (it will fail verification but we can see the flow)\n    # Actually, let's just verify the logic by reading the code\n    \n    # The key insight: after processing all anchors, ranges contains (from_seq, to_seq, commit) \n    # for each valid sigstore anchor. My fix checks that for each to_seq, the .ots file exists.\n    \n    print('The fix checks: for each range head (to_seq), anchors/{to_seq:06d}.ots must exist')\n    print('This is exactly what the bug report requested')\nfinally:\n    shutil.rmtree(d)\n\"","time":{"start":1794567573174,"end":1794567573222}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me write a more comprehensive test that simulates the exact scenario: a ledger with valid sigstore attestation but missing .ots file. Since we can't easily create a valid sigstore bundle, let me trace through the code logic to make sure my fix is correct.\n\nActually, looking at the code flow:\n1. `cmd_check_anchors` iterates through all anchor files\n2. For each `.sigstore.json`, it calls `check_sigstore` which verifies the bundle\n3. If verification passes, it extracts the range and adds to `ranges` list\n4. After processing all anchors, it sorts ranges and checks coverage\n5. My fix adds a check for `.ots` file existence for each range head\n\nThe fix looks correct. Let me verify by checking that the test `test_check_anchors_requires_the_writer_to_cover_every_entry` still works - it expects exit code 1 when there are no anchors at all.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1d4900eb2001boW5qieG8aLrdr","sessionID":"ses_e2b7162e1ffepACOlFctQOU9iv","messageID":"msg_1d48f74a3001lYk5LnEY01qshG"}}
